# XSH vfscanf(), vscanf(), vsscanf()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Each test calls the function from a variadic wrapper that does
# va_start(), the call and va_end(), as the page's APPLICATION USAGE
# advises; ap is not used again after the call (its value is unspecified).

assertion  XSH.vfscanf.001
anchor     functions/vfscanf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       vfscanf() behaves as fscanf() with the arguments taken from the
           va_list: on a stream reading "12 ab rest", vfscanf(f, "%d %s",
           ap) returns 2, stores 12 and "ab", and the next fgetc() returns
           the space after "ab".
test       t/001-vfscanf.c

assertion  XSH.vfscanf.002
anchor     functions/vfscanf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       vscanf() reads from stdin: with descriptor 0 open on a file
           holding "42 x", vscanf("%d", ap) returns 1 and stores 42, and
           the next getchar() returns the space.
test       t/002-vscanf.c

assertion  XSH.vfscanf.003
anchor     functions/vfscanf.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/vfscanf.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       vsscanf() reads from the string as sscanf() does: "7 8" with
           "%d %d" returns 2 with 7 and 8; "" with "%d" returns EOF; "5"
           with "%d%d" returns 1.
test       t/003-vsscanf.c

assertion  XSH.vfscanf.004
anchor     functions/vfscanf.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       Numbered conversions take their argument from the va_list:
           vsscanf("10 20", "%2$d %1$d", ap) with ap holding &a, &b
           returns 2 with a 20 and b 10.
note       The "%n$" form moved from the XSI option to the base in Issue 7.
test       t/004-numbered.c

assertion  XSH.vfscanf.005
anchor     functions/vfscanf.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/vfscanf.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       vfscanf() reports errors as fscanf() does: on a read stream
           whose descriptor has been replaced by a write-only one,
           vfscanf(f, "%d", ap) returns EOF with errno EBADF and ferror()
           true.
test       t/005-error.c
